An Older Book

The lighthouse keeper kept a ledger of every ship that passed, though no one had asked him to and no one ever read it.

Each morning he climbed the hundred and twelve steps with a pot of tea in one hand and the ledger under his arm, and each evening he climbed them again to light the lamp.

In the spring a boy from the village began to bring the post on Thursdays. He was eleven, and he wanted to know what the ledger was for.

The keeper said it was for remembering. The boy said that the ships remembered themselves, and the keeper allowed that this was probably true.
